# Parallel Plans Workflow

Codifies how to spin up N isolated worktrees, one per plan, so several Cursor agents can work in parallel on the same machine without file, port, or `.dart_tool/` collisions, then merge cleanly per `parallel-agents.mdc`.

## When to use worktrees vs alternatives

Pick the smallest tool that fits the task. Do not spin up worktrees for a single one-shot slice.

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Two or more plans need long, interactive Cursor iteration on the same machine (typical case) | **Git worktrees + local Cursor windows** (this rule) — one worktree per plan, one Cursor window per worktree, ports allocated by slot |
| Fire-and-forget slice; no live iteration needed and results can be reviewed after the fact | **`Task` subagent with `environment: "cloud"`** — runs on its own VM, own branch, own worktree; parent merges via MR afterwards |
| Multiple attempts at the **same** problem (best-of-N experiments, spike variants) | **Built-in `best-of-n-runner` subagent** — each attempt gets its own isolated git worktree and branch automatically |

Rule of thumb: worktrees for humans-in-the-loop, cloud subagents for autonomous slices, `best-of-n-runner` for parallel attempts at one problem.

## Three-layer branch model

Per-plan branches never target `main` directly. They stack under a parent-owned integration branch.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Main[main]
  Integration["feat/sprint-N-integration"]
  PlanA["feat/plan-a"]
  PlanB["feat/plan-b"]
  PlanC["feat/plan-c"]
  Integration -->|MR after Tier 1 + Tier 2| Main
  PlanA -->|MR after Tier 1| Integration
  PlanB -->|MR after Tier 1| Integration
  PlanC -->|MR after Tier 1| Integration
```

- **`main`** — protected; only integration MRs land here.
- **`feat/sprint-N-integration`** — parent-owned; all shared-file patches applied here (never on per-plan branches).
- **Per-plan branches** — base on **integration**, not `main`. Naming per `git-hygiene.mdc` (`<type>/<plan-slug>`).

## Port allocation table

Fixed slot → port map. Slot 0 is reserved for the main worktree; only claim slots 1..5 from the helper script.

| Slot | Worktree | FLUTTER_PORT | DOCKER_PORT |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `C:/NAGP/flo-compass` (main) | 3000 | 8080 |
| 1 | `../flo-compass-<slug>` | 3001 | 8081 |
| 2 | `../flo-compass-<slug>` | 3002 | 8082 |
| 3 | `../flo-compass-<slug>` | 3003 | 8083 |
| 4 | `../flo-compass-<slug>` | 3004 | 8084 |
| 5 | `../flo-compass-<slug>` | 3005 | 8085 |

**Cap at slot 5.** A dev box runs out of RAM before it runs out of port slots; higher counts should use `environment: "cloud"` subagents.

Each worktree gets a `.worktree.env` (git-excluded, never committed) that the helper script writes. `source .worktree.env` before `bash scripts/wsl_dev.sh` or `docker compose up` so ports resolve to the slot's assignment.

Sanity check before any dev tier: `echo $FLUTTER_PORT $DOCKER_PORT` — if empty, you forgot to source and will collide with the main worktree.

## Lifecycle scripts

Guided automation for per-plan finish → merge → cleanup → sprint close-out. Implementation in `scripts/wsl_plan_*.sh` with Windows launchers. Full reference: [scripts/README.md](../../scripts/README.md).

| Stage | Script (WSL) | Launcher (Windows) | Run from |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Start** | `wsl_new_plan_worktree.sh` | `new-plan-worktree.cmd` | Main worktree |
| **Finish** | `wsl_plan_worktree_finish.sh` | `plan-worktree-finish.cmd` | Per-plan worktree |
| **Merge** | `wsl_plan_worktree_merge.sh` | `plan-worktree-merge.cmd` | Main worktree |
| **Cleanup** | `wsl_plan_worktree_cleanup.sh` | `plan-worktree-cleanup.cmd` | Main worktree |
| **Sprint integrate** | `wsl_sprint_integrate.sh` | `sprint-integrate.cmd` | Main worktree (integration branch) |

### When to run each

1. **Start** — before dispatching a parallel plan agent (`new-plan-worktree.cmd feat/plan-x 1 --base feat/sprint-N-integration`). Writes `.cursor/plan-session.json` + `.worktree.env`.
2. **Finish** — when the per-plan agent completes: Tier 1, clean tree, forbidden-file gate, secret scan. Sets `ready: true` in `plan-session.json`.
3. **Merge** — parent on main worktree merges the ready branch into **integration** (not `main`). Uses `flock` on `.git/integration-merge.lock`, grep guardrails, Tier 1 on integration.
4. **Cleanup** — after merge: stop ports, `git worktree remove`, delete branch, free slot.
5. **Sprint integrate** — after all per-plan merges: full Tier 1 + Tier 2 + coverage on integration.

### Lock and `--resume`

- Only one integration merge at a time (`flock` + stale-lock auto-clear after 6 h or dead PID).
- If the plan queued **serial-merge patch notes**, merge pauses after Tier 1; parent applies patches on integration, commits, then re-runs `plan-worktree-merge.cmd <branch> --resume --yes`.
- `--dry-run` on merge/cleanup/integrate prints planned actions with no side effects.

### MR to main

Merge into integration only. Open **one MR: integration → `main`** manually after `sprint-integrate.cmd` passes Tier 1 + Tier 2.

### Rollback recipes

- **Merge failure:** `git merge --abort` or `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD; git reflog show <integration>`
- **Push failure after merge:** `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD@{1}`
- **Cleanup mistake:** `git checkout -b <branch> <sha-from-reflog>` + `git worktree add ../flo-compass-<slug> <branch>`
- **Integrate Tier 2 failure:** `docker compose down; fuser -k ${DOCKER_PORT}/tcp; git status`

Structured event log: `.cursor/plan-lifecycle.log.jsonl` (git-excluded; rotate manually at 10 MB or 30 days).

### Cursor hook reminders (opt-in)

`.cursor/hooks/plan-lifecycle-reminder.sh` nudges worktree start/finish. Two-layer control:

1. **Registration** — `.cursor/hooks.json` presence controls whether Cursor registers the hook. The file is git-ignored (per-worktree); the tracked source is `.cursor/hooks.json.template`. The main worktree has no `hooks.json` → no hook fires → no popup. `new-plan-worktree.cmd` copies the template into each new plan worktree automatically.
2. **Content** — `.cursor/plan-lifecycle.json` `enabled` controls whether the hook emits reminder text (secondary switch).

| Config | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `.cursor/hooks.json.template` | Tracked source of truth for hook registration |
| `.cursor/hooks.json` | Per-worktree registration (git-excluded; copied by `new-plan-worktree.cmd`) |
| `.cursor/plan-lifecycle.json` | Committed default (`"enabled": false`) |
| `.cursor/plan-lifecycle.local.json` | Per-developer override (git-excluded) |
| `PLAN_LIFECYCLE_ENABLED=1` | Env override for a single Cursor session |

**Enable reminders in a plan worktree:** run `new-plan-worktree.cmd` (copies `hooks.json.template` and writes `enabled: true` in `plan-lifecycle.json`).

**Pre-existing plan worktrees** (created before this change): run once inside that worktree:

```bash
cp .cursor/hooks.json.template .cursor/hooks.json
```

## Ownership contract mandate

Every plan intended for parallel dispatch **must** start from [.cursor/plans/_TEMPLATE_parallel_plan.md](.cursor/plans/_TEMPLATE_parallel_plan.md) and include:

- **Ownership contract header** — agent id, branch, worktree path, slot, `FLUTTER_PORT`, `DOCKER_PORT`
- **Files this agent MAY write** — exact paths or narrow globs
- **Files this agent MUST NOT touch** — pre-filled from `parallel-agents.mdc` forbidden list
- **Serial-merge patch notes** — subsection listing every change the parent must apply to a forbidden shared file on the integration branch; the per-plan agent describes the patch but never applies it

Missing contract → run the slices serially instead (`parallel-agents.mdc`).

## Serial-merge protocol

Follow `parallel-agents.mdc` lines 33-44 for the grep checks. Layered flow:

1. Each per-plan agent completes on its own branch, passes Tier 1 in-worktree, opens MR against `feat/sprint-N-integration`.
2. Parent reviews per-plan MR: confirms none of the forbidden shared files were touched on the per-plan branch; reads every serial-merge patch note the plan queued.
3. Parent merges per-plan branches into integration one at a time; after each merge runs the `parallel-agents.mdc` grep checks (routes, providers, manifest, log).
4. Parent applies all queued shared-file patches on the integration branch — never on per-plan branches, never as a background subagent.
5. Parent runs full WSL Tier 1 + Tier 2 on integration per `local-wsl-auto-validation.mdc`, then opens one MR: integration → `main`.

## Cleanup

After a per-plan branch merges into integration:

```bash
git worktree remove ../flo-compass-<slug>
git branch -d feat/plan-<slug>
```

The port slot is now free for reuse. This also keeps `git-hygiene.mdc`'s **"never end a session with more than 20 untracked files"** rule honest — the removed worktree cleans its own `build/` and `.dart_tool/`, and there is no orphaned `.worktree.env` to leak into the next slot.

## Do not

- Write auto-merge scripts — merge stays manual per `parallel-agents.mdc` and `git-hygiene.mdc`.
- Edit forbidden shared files (`parallel-agents.mdc`) on a per-plan branch; queue them as serial-merge patch notes instead.
- Run Tier 2 (Docker) simultaneously in more than one worktree unless `docker-compose.yml` uses `${DOCKER_PORT:-8080}` for the host port.
- Skip `source .worktree.env` — `wsl_dev.sh` will fall back to the main worktree's ports and collide.

## Lifecycle scripts

The three-layer branch model is enforced by five lifecycle scripts. Each has a matching Windows launcher (`.cmd` / `.ps1`) that mirrors [dev-local.cmd](../../dev-local.cmd) / [deploy-local.cmd](../../deploy-local.cmd) style — CRLF self-heal, `wsl -e bash -lc "cd /mnt/c/NAGP/flo-compass && bash scripts/<name>.sh ..."`, exit code passthrough via `if errorlevel 1 exit /b 1`.

| Script | Run from | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `wsl_new_plan_worktree.sh` (start) | Main worktree | Allocate slot, create worktree + branch off integration, write `.worktree.env` + `.cursor/plan-session.json`, seed plan file from template |
| `wsl_plan_worktree_finish.sh` (finish) | Per-plan worktree | Tier 1 gate + dirty-tree gate + forbidden-file gate + secret scan + patch-notes parser; flips session `ready:true` on pass |
| `wsl_plan_worktree_merge.sh` (merge) | Main worktree | Serialized `flock` merge into integration with pre-flight, reflog snapshot, grep guardrails, Tier 1 on integration, `--resume` for patch-note re-validation |
| `wsl_plan_worktree_cleanup.sh` (cleanup) | Main worktree | Merged-gate + unpushed-commit guard; stop worktree services, `git worktree remove`, `git branch -D`, `git worktree prune`; frees the port slot |
| `wsl_sprint_integrate.sh` (sprint-integrate) | Main worktree on integration | Full Tier 1 + Tier 2 + coverage gate on integration |

### CLI conventions (all four new lifecycle scripts)

- `-h` / `--help` — usage banner
- `--version` — script version + `git log -1 --format=%h scripts/`
- `--dry-run` (merge / cleanup / sprint-integrate only) — prints planned actions with `[dry-run]` prefix; no filesystem, git, or docker side effects
- `--yes` — non-interactive confirmation
- Script-specific: `<branch>` for merge and cleanup, `--base <integration>`, `--resume` (merge), `--force` (cleanup — allows discarding unpushed commits), `--force-behind` (merge — allows merging when integration is behind remote)

`sprint-integrate.cmd` additionally fires a `msg %USERNAME% /TIME:10 "..." 2>NUL` completion toast on Windows; suppress via `PLAN_LIFECYCLE_QUIET=1`. `2>NUL` also silences the toast on managed devices where `msg` is disabled.

### When to run each

- **Per-plan (once per branch)**: start → work → finish → merge → cleanup. Each per-plan agent stops at finish; merge and cleanup run from the main worktree by the parent.
- **Sprint-end (once per integration branch)**: after every per-plan branch is merged, run sprint-integrate. It is the only step that runs Tier 2 + coverage.

### Merge lock and `--resume`

`wsl_plan_worktree_merge.sh` acquires `.git/integration-merge.lock` via `flock -w 30` (fallback: atomic `mkdir .git/integration-merge.lockdir`) so two operators cannot merge into integration simultaneously. The lockfile embeds `PID`, `HOST`, `STARTED_AT`, and `COMMAND`; if `HOST` matches and either the PID is dead or `STARTED_AT` age > 6 h, the merge script auto-clears the lock and retries once. A `trap 'lifecycle_cleanup' INT TERM EXIT` releases the lock on Ctrl+C and records an `interrupted` event in the log.

When a plan has serial-merge patch notes (see `parallel-agents.mdc` "Mandatory serial merge step"), merge halts after the guardrails pass and prompts the operator to apply the patches on integration. Rerun with `--resume` after committing them; `--resume` skips the merge step (already merged) and only re-runs the guardrails and Tier 1.

### Merge into integration, not `main`

Every lifecycle script targets `feat/sprint-N-integration`, never `main`. The MR from integration to `main` is **manual** and is opened only after `sprint-integrate` reports Tier 1 + Tier 2 + coverage green. There is no auto-MR and no auto-merge to `main`.

### Rollback recipes

Each script's success/failure banner prints the exact rollback command relevant to its stage. Summary (full details in [scripts/README.md](../../scripts/README.md)):

- **merge failure**: `git merge --abort` or `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD; git reflog show ${base}`
- **push failure after merge**: `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD@{1}` (before the merge commit)
- **cleanup mistake**: `git checkout -b ${branch} <sha-from-reflog>` and `git worktree add ../flo-compass-<slug> ${branch}`
- **sprint-integrate Tier 2 failure**: `docker compose down; fuser -k ${DOCKER_PORT}/tcp; git status`
- **stale merge lock (manual override)**: `cat .git/integration-merge.lock` (inspect ownership), then `rm .git/integration-merge.lock` if no other merge is running

Every phase transition emits a JSON line to `.cursor/plan-lifecycle.log.jsonl` (git-excluded, append-only, manual rotation at 10 MB or 30 days) so a bad merge can be reconstructed. See [scripts/README.md](../../scripts/README.md) for the full field reference.

